WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.920
White dwarfs can appear yellow, pink, or purple when trace elements in their dense atmospheres filter the light they emit. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 232. Valas Marineris still shifting, Mylar stacked for the moon, and a compact core in the Dragon Cloud follow. Researchers at the University of Warwick modeled 151 white dwarfs within 20 par sex of Earth, according to Universe Today. Five of those stars, roughly 3.3% display non-white colors

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.160
when carbon, calcium, or sodium sits in their extremely dense atmospheres.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:40.160
Carbon produces pink or purple hues, while hydrogen and helium at lower temperatures yield

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.640
cyan or blue tones.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:45.600
Debris from destroyed planets leave some white dwarfs looking green.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:51.120
The team notes that the median white dwarf across a representative population remains white.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.900
Dr. Mark Hollins, the study's lead author, explained that a true black body can only appear

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:04.000
red, orange, white, or pale blue. At 6,000 Kelvin most radiation spreads across optical wavelengths

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:09.820
and the object looks white, and hotter objects shift toward ultraviolet and appear pale blue.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:16.340
Dr. Boris Gansica, a co-author, noted that 1 in 30 may not sound like a huge fraction,

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.280
yet across the entire population of white dwarfs that still represents a lot of oddly colored stars.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:28.520
The findings were published in RIS techniques and instruments. White dwarfs are the end state

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:33.960
of most stars, so even that small rate implies a very large number of oddly colored remnants

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.300
across the galaxy. Color anomalies offer a way to read surface composition without needing

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:44.880
direct spectra of every target. Wide field surveys could use those anomalies as quick flags

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:51.068
for stars that once hosted planets now destroyed. Valas marinaris continues to shape the surface

00:01:51.068 --> 00:01:55.728
of Mars, according to scientists at Imperial College London and Columbia University.

00:01:56.348 --> 00:02:00.228
The work shows ongoing fault motion long after the canyon first formed.

00:02:00.868 --> 00:02:03.508
Tectonic processes remain active on Mars today.

00:02:04.628 --> 00:02:08.988
Penn engineers stacked hundreds of everyday mylar sheets to create a durable, lightweight

00:02:08.988 --> 00:02:14.068
insulator suited to the moon's extreme temperature swings, as reported by Fizz.org.

00:02:14.528 --> 00:02:19.068
The material is designed to protect future habitats from hundreds of degrees of variation

00:02:19.068 --> 00:02:24.288
between lunar day and night. The layered approach provides both thermal protection and mechanical

00:02:24.288 --> 00:02:30.795
flexibility for habitat construction. Alma found an exceptionally compact core inside the infrared

00:02:30.795 --> 00:02:37.095
dark dragon cloud that may mark the earliest stage of massive star formation. The core was identified

00:02:37.095 --> 00:02:42.415
in observations accepted for astronomy and astrophysics. The paper was posted to the archive

00:02:42.415 --> 00:02:48.382
pre-print server on October 5th. Radiation from the massive central star Theta1 Orion

00:02:48.382 --> 00:02:54.422
is C1, strips gas and dust from planet-forming discs around younger stars, sculpting them

00:02:54.422 --> 00:02:59.502
into tadpole shapes. A new study suggests a past stellar collision helps set the conditions

00:02:59.502 --> 00:03:04.722
that produce these distinctive forms. The Orion Nebula is a large star-forming region

00:03:04.722 --> 00:03:10.561
visible to the naked eye. SETI, the search for extraterrestrial intelligence, is placing

00:03:10.561 --> 00:03:15.981
a pair of laser-setty optical sensors in Saudi Arabia to expand the global network hunting

00:03:15.981 --> 00:03:18.741
for laser pulses from technological civilizations.

00:03:19.301 --> 00:03:24.381
The instruments described in universe today will join an existing array designed to detect

00:03:24.381 --> 00:03:28.381
brief flashes of light that could indicate extraterrestrial technology.

00:03:29.317 --> 00:03:33.977
A composite photograph records Orionid meteors produced as Earth passed through the dust

00:03:33.977 --> 00:03:35.517
trail left by Halley's comet.

00:03:35.517 --> 00:03:41.157
The image, covered by space.com, highlights the annual shower's connection to the famous

00:03:41.157 --> 00:03:42.017
comments orbit.

00:03:42.939 --> 00:03:47.879
Black holes spinning near their maximum rate are the subject of a theoretical study, asking

00:03:47.879 --> 00:03:50.179
whether one could split into two smaller ones.

00:03:50.659 --> 00:03:55.019
The work was published in the journal Physical Review D under the title when Black holes

00:03:55.019 --> 00:03:58.759
admit pair of pants topologies, according to Fizz.org.

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:04.199
The calculations examine extreme spin conditions where standard merger outcomes might reverse.

00:04:04.199 --> 00:04:11.411
Elsewhere, Lazuli Space Telescope researchers have selected 27 exoplanets, including several

00:04:11.411 --> 00:04:15.711
well-studied worlds, for early observations once the instrument reaches orbit.

00:04:16.251 --> 00:04:21.831
A Falcon 9 lifted 27 Starlink satellites from Vandenberg Space Force Base, marking a

00:04:21.831 --> 00:04:25.231
slower launch cadence as the company shifts focus towards Starship.

00:04:26.172 --> 00:04:31.132
White dwarfs pack the mass of the Sun into a volume the size of Earth, so their atmospheres

00:04:31.132 --> 00:04:33.432
are thousands of times denser than the Sun's.

00:04:33.432 --> 00:04:38.852
In ordinary stars, the light we see follows a smooth black-body curve set only by temperature,

00:04:39.332 --> 00:04:43.332
yet the new models show that a few percent of white dwarfs deviate from that curve.

00:04:43.932 --> 00:04:49.152
Add carbon at the same 6,000 Kelvin and the tint turns pink or purple, because the

00:04:49.152 --> 00:04:51.992
element absorbs and re-amits light at specific bands.

00:04:53.115 --> 00:04:58.055
Models treat the stars as black bodies, whose emission depends only on temperature, yet

00:04:58.055 --> 00:05:00.375
the added elements shift the observed spectrum.

00:05:00.375 --> 00:05:05.535
From dense atmospheres allow trace elements to alter that pattern in ways impossible for

00:05:05.535 --> 00:05:06.635
main-sequence stars.

00:05:07.155 --> 00:05:12.235
The same dense layers also trap debris from destroyed planets, and green tones appear

00:05:12.235 --> 00:05:14.435
when that material settles into the atmosphere.

00:05:15.115 --> 00:05:20.615
Cooler white dwarfs with hydrogen and helium tend towards cyan or blue, because those gases

00:05:20.615 --> 00:05:22.775
suppress certain parts of the visible range.

00:05:23.275 --> 00:05:27.915
These effects only appear because the atmospheres are so compressed that even parts per million

00:05:27.915 --> 00:05:30.915
and abundances of heavy elements become optically important.

00:05:31.495 --> 00:05:35.975
In a normal star, the same trace amounts would be invisible against the much lower density.

00:05:36.675 --> 00:05:41.755
The team's computer models treat the atmosphere as a stratified layer where pressure, temperature

00:05:41.755 --> 00:05:43.935
and composition interact at every depth.

00:05:44.595 --> 00:05:49.035
Small changes in the abundance of carbon can swing the output from pink to purple depending

00:05:49.035 --> 00:05:50.275
on the exact temperature.

00:05:50.935 --> 00:05:55.495
The five stars in the nearby sample already demonstrate that the phenomenon is repeatable

00:05:55.495 --> 00:05:57.115
rather than a modeling artifact.

00:05:57.695 --> 00:06:02.495
Because the color shift depends on both temperature and composition, observers can now

00:06:02.495 --> 00:06:07.735
use broadband filters to flag candidates for follow-up spectroscopy, instead of examining

00:06:07.735 --> 00:06:09.235
every white dwarf in detail.

00:06:09.935 --> 00:06:14.495
Future observations with larger telescopes could measure the actual spectra of these objects

00:06:14.495 --> 00:06:17.395
and confirm whether the predicted colors match reality.

00:06:17.875 --> 00:06:23.075
If they do, color surveys could become an efficient first filter for identifying white dwarfs

00:06:23.075 --> 00:06:24.295
that once hosted planets.

00:06:24.295 --> 00:06:29.295
The remaining puzzle is whether these colored stars represent a brief transitional phase

00:06:29.295 --> 00:06:34.515
or a stable end state, and whether the same process operates in white dwarfs that formed

00:06:34.515 --> 00:06:35.455
through different channels.

00:06:36.015 --> 00:06:40.475
We can already see the color difference in the models, but we do not yet know how often

00:06:40.475 --> 00:06:44.975
the responsible elements arrive from outside versus how often they are dredged up from the

00:06:44.975 --> 00:06:46.015
star's own interior.

00:06:46.555 --> 00:06:50.895
The open question is how long these color signatures persist before the elements sink

00:06:50.895 --> 00:06:51.915
or are mixed away.

00:06:51.915 --> 00:06:57.635
Some white dwarfs may show the effect only during a narrow window after a planetary disruption event.

00:06:58.155 --> 00:07:01.955
Others could maintain the color for much longer if the elements remain suspended.

00:07:02.615 --> 00:07:07.075
Resolving that time scale would tell us whether the colored population is a steady fraction of

00:07:07.075 --> 00:07:13.666
all white dwarfs, or a transient phase that most stars pass through. Once the laser-setty stations in

00:07:13.666 --> 00:07:18.826
Saudi Arabia begin observing, the network will have added optical coverage over a new region of

00:07:18.826 --> 00:07:20.266
sky for those pulse searches.

00:07:21.006 --> 00:07:26.046
First light from the Lazuli Space Telescope will show what its 27 chosen exoplanets look

00:07:26.046 --> 00:07:28.506
like from a private observatory in orbit.

